# What survives when a paper leaves the lab: re-testing {{papers}} robot-learning papers on a €{{rig_eur}} rig, and auditing our own pre-registration

*Ravindu Somawansa, Shisso Robotics. Draft, not submitted.*

## Abstract

Robot-learning papers are, with few exceptions, demonstrated in simulation or on hardware far more capable than what a small lab can afford, and it is seldom straightforward to tell from a paper which of its claims would survive the move to a cheaper rig. We re-tested {{c_paper}} claims from {{papers}} recent papers on an SO-101 arm driven by a Jetson Orin NX, a rig whose parts cost €{{rig_eur}} and whose compute cost ${{cost}}. For each claim we ran the cheapest baseline first and committed the pass mark to version control before the test. We find that {{c_paper_repro}} of the {{c_paper}} claims hold on this hardware. The failures fall into three classes that we propose as a working taxonomy: methods that miss the real-time budget on the edge GPU, zero-shot judges that lose to a trivial signal, and latent planners that lose to the commanded joints. Because a register of this kind is only as good as the discipline behind it, we also audit our own pre-registration by reading the commit history of every gate. For most gates the bar provably precedes the result; for some the history cannot show the order, and we report those as well.

## 1. The register

![Fig. 1 — what survived when the papers left the lab: of the {{c_paper}} paper claims, {{c_paper_repro}} reproduce and {{c_paper_fail}} fail; the failures fall into three classes (the clock, zero-shot judges against a trivial signal, learned latents against the commanded joints); our own gates have the bar committed before the result in {{pr_bar_first}} of {{pr_rows}} and the result first in {{pr_result_first}}.](fig/verdicts.png)

Our rig is an SO-101 arm and a Jetson Orin NX 16 GB. Most of the {{papers}} papers in this study were demonstrated on setups far from it: {{rig_sim}} were shown in simulation and {{rig_gt20k}} on rigs above $20k, whereas only {{rig_lt5k}} used a setup under $5k, which is where our rig falls. The appendix table gives the rig of each paper, including the {{rig_5to20k}} in between and the {{rig_unstated}} that does not say.

The register grew out of our own write-ups. Each re-test on this rig was published with its data, and there are {{l0}} such write-ups so far. This paper collects them in one table and, in addition, audits how the table was kept.

To make the format concrete, consider one entry. An adaptive chunking paper claims that choosing the chunk length from the entropy of sampled chunks follows the phases of the task, long in transport and short at the grasp. On our edge GPU one such decision takes {{ex_a_ours}}, against a budget of {{ex_a_bar}}, so the entry records a fail on this rig; it says nothing about the method on the hardware it was shown on. Next to the verdict, the entry keeps the claim in the paper's own words, our pass mark, our measurement and the published rows.

For each claim we first run the cheapest signal that the learned model has to beat, on the same rows. For a predictor this is copy-last; for a latent it is the commanded joints; for a judge it is episode length. This ordering matters, because a learned method that cannot beat a trivial signal on the same data has not yet shown what it claims, whatever its absolute score.

We chose the papers for the stack this rig runs (world models, VLA policies, failure monitors, chunking, edge latency), so the failure rate describes this register and not the field. The register file lists every paper we have tested and every paper scheduled next, entered before its test.

## 2. Auditing our own pre-registration

A gate's pass mark had to be in version control before its result. Notes and commit dates can be edited by hand, but commit ancestry cannot be changed without rewriting history that others have already cloned, so ancestry is what we audit. For each gate we find the first commit that contains its bar and the first that contains its result, and compare them (`repro/prereg-check/scripts/prereg_audit.py`, abridged):

```python
def ancestor(a, b):
    return subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(RR), "merge-base", "--is-ancestor", a, b]).returncode == 0
...
            elif b[0] == res[0]:
                cls = "same-commit"
            elif ancestor(b[0], res[0]):
                cls = "bar-first"
            elif ancestor(res[0], b[0]):
                cls = "result-first"
```

Table 1 gives the classes for the {{pr_rows}} gates that ran and were logged by 2026-10-31. Most are bar-first and {{pr_result_first}} are result-first. A same-commit gate is one where bar and result entered the repository together; it says nothing about which was written first. The log holds {{pr_cancelled}} further gates that are not in the table because we did not run them: one was superseded by a later test, and the other was dropped because neither of its outcomes would have changed what we built.

Table 1: Commit-order class of each gate. The audited column is the one the paper uses; the first pass is what text search alone gave on the same gates.

| class | first text pass | audited, 2026-10-31 | audited, today |
|---|---|---|---|
| bar-first | {{v1_bar_first}} | {{pr_bar_first}} | {{pt_bar_first}} |
| same-commit | {{v1_same_commit}} | {{pr_same_commit}} | {{pt_same_commit}} |
| result-first | — | {{pr_result_first}} | {{pt_result_first}} |
| no bar | {{v1_no_bar}} | {{pr_no_bar}} | {{pt_no_bar}} |
| unresolved | {{v1_unresolved}} | — | — |
| gates | {{pr_rows}} | {{pr_rows}} | {{pr_today}} |

The first pass used text search alone and found {{v1_same_commit}} same-commit gates, an artefact of batch logging. For each of them we went back to the earlier file that holds its bar: {{sc_to_bf}} gates moved to bar-first and {{sc_to_nb}} to no-bar, because their bar cell declares none, while {{sc_stay}} stayed same-commit. The audit table in the repository gives the evidence line for every move. We did not re-audit the gates that were already bar-first. One case is still open: the reward-model progress test of 2026-11-01, which text search calls same-commit although its protocol (`{{top_protocol}}`) is older than the log commit.

The early gates mixed point estimates with intervals of different kinds. We therefore recomputed {{rc_rows}} of them from stored rows with one statistic, the episode bootstrap ({{rc_resamples}} resamples, percentile 95 % CI). For {{rc_flips}} of them the bar falls inside the interval (Table 2). The colour judge is a {{acc_verdict}} on the point estimate, but its interval does not settle the question; the false-intervention threshold was fitted to meet its rate, so that interval is descriptive only. The register keeps the pre-registered point verdicts and marks these three as fragile.

Table 2: The three gates whose verdict flips somewhere inside its 95 % interval.

| gate | n | estimate | 95 % CI | bar | point verdict |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| colour judge (no learning), accuracy | {{acc_n}} | {{acc_est}} % | {{acc_lo}}–{{acc_hi}} % | ≥ {{acc_bar}} % | {{acc_verdict}} |
| oscillation among failed episodes | {{osc_n}} | {{osc_est}} % | {{osc_lo}}–{{osc_hi}} % | ≥ {{osc_bar}} % | {{osc_verdict}} |
| judge threshold, false interventions | {{tau_n}} | {{tau_est}} % | {{tau_lo}}–{{tau_hi}} % | ≤ {{tau_bar}} % | {{tau_verdict}} |

All {{l0}} write-ups rebuild from their published rows. The check for the cold reruns prints:

```text
$ python3 repro/cold-reproduction/l0_check.py
ok  posts: 29 (report 29)
ok  L0 PASS: 29 (report 29)
ok  L1 REPRO: 6 (report 6)
ok  L1 DIFF: 0 (report 0)
ok  L1 classes: {'REPRO': 6, '—': 3, 'SKIP': 8, 'FAIL-ENV': 12}
L0 PASS
```

We ran the cold reruns from a fresh clone with no credentials. Of the gates, {{l1_repro}} reran and matched, {{l1_exact}} of them exactly, and {{l1_env}} more stop at the world-model environment, which is not yet published; publishing it would unblock most of them. The remaining {{l1_skip}} require a larger GPU, a private checkpoint or the arm, or depend on a step the write-up leaves out. We also checked the register's citations and paper-side fields against the papers' PDFs and corrected them. The register names {{ps_ids}} arXiv ids; the ids missing from our papers mirror all belong to scheduled tests, and one tested paper has no arXiv id, so it could not be checked against a PDF.

