## 4. Three failure classes

The failures we observed are not of one kind, and we found it useful to distinguish three. The adaptive chunking entry of §1 misses the real-time budget on the edge GPU; the method may well work on faster hardware, and the failure is one of the clock. We call this class A.

In the second kind of failure, a model judges outcomes without having been trained on this rig. We ran a learned reward model as a failure detector and it reached an AUROC of {{ex_b_ours}}, whereas episode length alone reaches {{ex_b_len}} on the same rollouts. Judges of success, failure or progress that lose to a trivial signal in this way form class B. The third kind concerns learned latents. A world model ranked the executed chunk first in {{ex_c_ours}} of anchors against a pass mark of {{ex_c_bar}}, while an action-only baseline reaches {{ex_c_base}}. Latents used to plan, rank or track progress that lose to the joints, to copy-last or to an action-only baseline form class C.

These examples illustrate the classes and do not count them. The classes are our proposal, drawn from the author's reading of the {{fail_rows}} failed rows, and we expect them to be refined as the register grows.

## 5. Limitations

The main confound in this study is observer bias: one operator, the author, ran the rig and labelled the outcomes, and labels were given at rest and not always blind to the arm. Where commit order shows it, the pass mark predates the run, so a label could not have moved the bar. For {{pr_unprov}} of the {{pr_rows}} gates, however, the repository cannot show whether bar or result came first, and {{sc_to_bf}} of the bar-first verdicts rest on matching a bar text in an earlier file. Under the bootstrap, {{rc_flips}} verdicts are fragile. Cold reruns cover {{l1_repro}} gates so far, with the unpublished world-model environment blocking most of the rest.

## 6. Related work

Several works evaluate policies on the same arm. A standardized real-world benchmark reports a taxonomy of failures and recoveries for VLA policies [@2606.08881], and a fleet of SO-101 cells evaluates policies at scale [@2607.24481]. The vla-eval tool runs VLAs across 14 simulation benchmarks [@2603.13966], and a benchmark of demonstration quality studies one method family [@2610.18869]. Our work differs in its object: we test the claims behind methods (judges, world models, latency) against thresholds fixed before the run, and we audit our own pre-registration. The closest work in spirit is RoboSeed [@2610.40026], which scores VLA and world-action models on five dimensions, including reproduction evidence and deployment affordability. Its reproduction-evidence score counts five audit milestones (configuration mapping, task records, task coverage, training records and seeds), and its authors are careful to state that the score is not a probability that a result is correct. In contrast, we give one pass or fail per claim on a €{{rig_eur}} rig. Concerns of this kind are not new to machine learning: the reproducibility of deep reinforcement learning results [@1709.06560] and the statistical fragility of point estimates reported from a handful of runs [@2108.13264] have been examined before, and our register can be read as the robot-learning counterpart of that line of work, with the added constraint that the hardware itself is part of what is being reproduced.

## 7. Conclusion

> **Conclusion.** We re-tested {{c_paper}} paper claims on a rig that cost €{{rig_eur}} in parts and ${{cost}} in compute, and {{c_paper_repro}} of them held. Every row behind the verdicts is published, and every number in this paper can be rebuilt from the repository (`github.com/ravediamond/paper-register-reproducibility`). For {{pr_unprov}} of {{pr_rows}} gates our own history cannot show the order of bar and result. Our findings suggest that re-testing a paper on a cheap rig should start with its cheapest baseline on the same rows, with the threshold committed before the run; we hope that the register and its audit offer a template for doing so.

